1
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:11,930
On 20 July 1969, the Apollo 11 lunar
module Eagle landed on the Moon, and

2
00:00:11,990 --> 00:00:14,510
Mission Control in Houston could finally
breathe.

3
00:00:15,070 --> 00:00:18,630
Tranquility Base here.
The Eagle has landed.

4
00:00:19,390 --> 00:00:21,090
The final minutes had been tense.

5
00:00:21,670 --> 00:00:25,550
The lander's computer raised an overload
alarm, and controllers in Houston had

6
00:00:25,610 --> 00:00:27,780
only seconds to decide whether to go on.

7
00:00:28,130 --> 00:00:32,800
They said go, and Neil Armstrong took
over by hand to fly past a crater and

8
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:35,260
boulders, with the fuel running low.

9
00:00:35,610 --> 00:00:39,650
A few hours later, a small television
camera on the side of the Eagle began

10
00:00:39,710 --> 00:00:42,980
sending pictures of Neil Armstrong
climbing down to the surface.

11
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:46,080
It was a black and white camera built by
Westinghouse.

12
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:50,550
Armstrong swung open its compartment as
he climbed down, and Buzz Aldrin

13
00:00:50,610 --> 00:00:52,710
switched it on from inside the lander.

14
00:00:53,070 --> 00:00:57,220
The picture was grainy, ghostly, and
black and white, and hundreds of

15
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:00,320
millions of people around the world
watched it as it happened.

16
00:01:01,570 --> 00:01:04,030
That's one small step for man,

17
00:01:06,770 --> 00:01:09,230
one giant leap for mankind.

18
00:01:09,570 --> 00:01:13,630
The walk lasted about two and a half
hours, and the camera followed Armstrong

19
00:01:13,690 --> 00:01:17,870
and Buzz Aldrin as they planted a flag,
read a plaque, and collected rock

20
00:01:17,930 --> 00:01:18,630
samples.

21
00:01:19,130 --> 00:01:22,570
Later in the walk, President Richard
Nixon telephoned the astronauts from the

22
00:01:22,630 --> 00:01:25,830
White House, and the call went out on
the same broadcast.

23
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:30,790
And this certainly has to be the most historic
telephone call ever made.

24
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:35,230
Those pictures still exist, and they are
some of the most famous television ever

25
00:01:35,290 --> 00:01:35,810
made.

26
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:40,640
What no one can find is the original
recording of that signal, made at the

27
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:43,000
tracking stations on magnetic tape.

28
00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:49,100
This is the story of a strange signal,
three radio dishes, a tape shortage, and

29
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:51,660
a search that ended in 2009.

30
00:01:55,030 --> 00:01:57,700
The camera did not send ordinary
television.

31
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:02,180
It sent slow scan video, ten frames a
second with three hundred and twenty

32
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:04,990
lines, which no home television could
show.

33
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:09,310
When it first switched on, the picture
was upside down, because the camera had

34
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:11,350
been mounted that way in its
compartment.

35
00:02:11,650 --> 00:02:15,580
Three tracking stations caught that
signal: Goldstone in California,

36
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:19,740
Honeysuckle Creek near Canberra, and the
Parkes radio telescope in New South

37
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:20,360
Wales.

38
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:24,970
At Goldstone nobody had flipped the
switch that turns the picture over, and

39
00:02:25,030 --> 00:02:28,060
for a while the pictures there also came
out as a negative.

40
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:32,690
At Honeysuckle Creek, a video engineer
saw the problem and turned the picture

41
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:34,990
the right way up in about twenty
seconds.

42
00:02:35,490 --> 00:02:39,720
At the stations, the slow scan picture
was converted to a standard television

43
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:43,570
picture, in effect by pointing a
television camera at a monitor.

44
00:02:43,870 --> 00:02:47,880
When the first picture of Armstrong on
the ladder reached Houston, the capsule

45
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:50,900
communicator told him that controllers
could see him.

46
00:02:52,130 --> 00:02:54,590
Okay, Neil, we can see you
coming down the ladder now.

47
00:02:55,150 --> 00:02:59,240
Just under nine minutes in, the Moon
moved into the main beam and the Parkes

48
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:00,360
picture arrived.

49
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:04,640
Houston briefly cut back to Goldstone to
compare, then returned to Parkes for

50
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:05,660
most of the walk.

51
00:03:06,070 --> 00:03:09,590
Houston's network controller told the
Parkes team that they had given everyone

52
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:11,020
the best television yet.

53
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:15,470
Australian viewers, around ten million
of them, watched the same pictures on

54
00:03:15,530 --> 00:03:16,270
the ABC.

55
00:03:16,890 --> 00:03:20,860
Alongside the broadcast, each station
also recorded the raw signal on

56
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:23,590
one-inch, fourteen-track magnetic tape.

57
00:03:23,870 --> 00:03:27,430
Those reels were sent to the Goddard
Space Flight Center in Maryland, and

58
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:30,590
they held a better record of the
moonwalk than the picture the networks

59
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:31,360
received.

60
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:35,260
That original signal had never been
through the conversion, which is why

61
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:38,750
engineers valued it, and why its loss
was felt later.

62
00:03:42,110 --> 00:03:46,210
NASA's later report estimated that about
forty-five reels held the moonwalk,

63
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:49,090
roughly fifteen from each of the three
stations.

64
00:03:49,590 --> 00:03:53,790
After the mission the tapes went into
storage, and by 1970 their records were

65
00:03:53,850 --> 00:03:56,990
filed at the Washington National Records
Center in Maryland.

66
00:03:57,450 --> 00:03:59,050
Nothing marked them as special.

67
00:03:59,350 --> 00:04:03,710
NASA's report found no sign that anyone
recognised the unique content on those

68
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:04,310
reels.

69
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:10,190
Then, in the early 1980s, NASA ran short
of one-inch magnetic tape, and Goddard

70
00:04:10,250 --> 00:04:13,940
recalled tens of thousands of boxes from
storage to be reused.

71
00:04:14,310 --> 00:04:18,680
Old tape could be wiped with a strong
magnet, a process called degaussing,

72
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:20,950
then checked and put back into service.

73
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:25,660
The report concludes that the moonwalk
reels were most likely degaussed and

74
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:29,890
reused that way, although it also allows
that they may have been thrown away.

75
00:04:30,310 --> 00:04:34,540
No one seems to have noticed what had
been lost until years later, when people

76
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:38,160
who remembered the mission began asking
where the originals had gone.

77
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:43,570
In 1997, John Sarkissian, who worked at
the Parkes radio telescope in Australia,

78
00:04:43,630 --> 00:04:45,800
began looking for the Australian
recordings.

79
00:04:46,290 --> 00:04:50,130
It began when a British author asked him
what had become of the originals.

80
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:55,190
Inside NASA, the search was led by
Richard Nafzger, a Goddard engineer who

81
00:04:55,250 --> 00:04:58,590
had worked on the Apollo television data
in 1969.

82
00:04:58,990 --> 00:05:02,700
Stan Lebar, who had managed the building
of the lunar television camera for

83
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:05,630
Westinghouse, also took part in the
effort.

84
00:05:06,070 --> 00:05:09,790
They went through shipping records,
storage sites, and tracking station

85
00:05:09,850 --> 00:05:12,700
files in the United States and in
Australia.

86
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:17,740
In 2002 the searchers heard about a tape
believed to hold Armstrong's first step.

87
00:05:18,130 --> 00:05:21,930
A former Honeysuckle Creek employee had
kept it in his garage in Canberra for

88
00:05:21,990 --> 00:05:23,250
more than thirty years.

89
00:05:23,750 --> 00:05:26,900
In June 2004 it was played at Goddard.

90
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:31,670
It held a simulation from October 1967,
not the moonwalk.

91
00:05:32,030 --> 00:05:35,900
The trail led to the records center in
Maryland, but of the twenty-six hundred

92
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:39,150
containers in that shipment, only two
were still there.

93
00:05:39,490 --> 00:05:44,410
In 2006 the search became public, and
Sarkissian published a detailed account

94
00:05:44,470 --> 00:05:46,530
of what he had found and what he had
not.

95
00:05:46,980 --> 00:05:52,690
On 16 July 2009, at a news conference in
Washington, NASA said the search was

96
00:05:52,750 --> 00:05:56,080
over and that the tapes most likely no
longer existed.

97
00:05:56,460 --> 00:06:00,730
Nafzger held up a reel of the same kind
of fourteen-track tape that had recorded

98
00:06:00,790 --> 00:06:03,530
the signal, a reminder of what had been
lost.

99
00:06:06,910 --> 00:06:09,220
But the moonwalk itself was not lost.

100
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:13,660
What disappeared was the best recording
of its signal, not the broadcast the

101
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:14,760
world had watched.

102
00:06:15,170 --> 00:06:19,770
NASA looked for the best surviving
copies and used three: a recording made

103
00:06:19,830 --> 00:06:24,800
in Sydney, broadcast tapes from the CBS
News archive, and film recordings stored

104
00:06:24,860 --> 00:06:26,240
at the Johnson Space Center.

105
00:06:26,710 --> 00:06:31,300
A company in Burbank, California, called
Lowry Digital, which usually restores

106
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:35,970
old Hollywood films, cleaned those
copies with digital processing, and NASA

107
00:06:36,030 --> 00:06:40,270
released fifteen restored scenes on 16
July 2009.

108
00:06:40,570 --> 00:06:45,010
The restored pictures are clearer than
what viewers saw in 1969, but they

109
00:06:45,070 --> 00:06:48,500
cannot add detail that the surviving
copies never held.

110
00:06:49,290 --> 00:06:53,940
Here is one of those scenes, first as it
was broadcast, and then restored.

111
00:06:54,630 --> 00:07:00,190
Okay. I'm going to leave that one foot up there
and both hands down to about the fourth rung up.

112
00:07:00,690 --> 00:07:04,080
Some people say the missing tapes prove
the landing was staged, but the

113
00:07:04,140 --> 00:07:08,420
broadcast survives, and tracking
stations in California and Australia

114
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:10,980
received the same signal at the same
time.

115
00:07:11,390 --> 00:07:14,110
NASA says no moonwalk footage is
missing.

116
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:18,840
Every slow scan picture was converted
live and sent to Houston and on to the

117
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:21,520
world, so only the station tapes are
gone.

118
00:07:21,890 --> 00:07:27,220
The real explanation is more ordinary: a
tape shortage, a routine recall of old

119
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:30,950
boxes, and a recording that nobody
realised was unique.

120
00:07:31,280 --> 00:07:33,060
There was a footnote in 2019.

121
00:07:33,420 --> 00:07:38,090
Sotheby's sold three videotapes labeled
Apollo 11 EVA for one point eight two

122
00:07:38,150 --> 00:07:39,050
million dollars.

123
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:43,890
NASA says they were most likely recorded
in Houston from the already converted

124
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:48,400
picture, and hold no footage it lacks,
so they are not the missing station

125
00:07:48,460 --> 00:07:48,960
tapes.

126
00:07:49,410 --> 00:07:55,060
In 1969 the world saw the moonwalk once,
live, and that live broadcast is still

127
00:07:55,120 --> 00:07:56,660
the version most people know.
